# Android Project Analyzer

Produce a full technical report by following these steps in order.

## Step 1 — Locate Project Root

Check for `settings.gradle`, `gradlew`, `build.gradle` / `build.gradle.kts` in the workspace root. If not found, ask the user to confirm the project root.

## Step 2 — Project Structure & Modules

Read `settings.gradle` or `settings.gradle.kts` to list all Gradle modules. Count `.kt` and `.java` source files. Map the top-level directory structure (2 levels, excluding `build/` and `.gradle/`).

## Step 3 — App Metadata

Read `app/src/main/AndroidManifest.xml`. Extract: `applicationId`, `minSdkVersion`, `targetSdkVersion`, `compileSdkVersion`, all permissions (classify Dangerous / Normal / Signature), exported components, `debuggable`, `allowBackup`, `usesCleartextTraffic`.

## Step 4 — Build Configuration & Dependencies

Read `gradle/libs.versions.toml` (version catalog), root `build.gradle[.kts]`, and `app/build.gradle[.kts]`. For every declared dependency extract: group, artifact, version, scope (implementation / api / testImplementation / etc.), and category (UI / Network / DI / Database / Testing / etc.). Also record: Kotlin version, AGP version, JVM target, build types, ProGuard/R8 status, buildFeatures (Compose, ViewBinding, DataBinding).

## Step 5 — Architecture Detection

Search source files for: ViewModel / StateFlow / LiveData (MVVM signals), UiState / Reducer / MVI signals, UseCase / Repository / DataSource (Clean Architecture layers), @HiltAndroidApp / startKoin / @Module (DI framework), NavController / NavHost (navigation), @Composable count (Compose vs View system).

## Step 6 — Feature Inventory

Identify features from: multi-module settings, feature-named packages, Activity subclasses, top-level Screen composables, deep link / route declarations.

## Step 7 — Code Quality Indicators

Count: unit test files, instrumented test files, TODO/FIXME markers, `!!` force-unwrap occurrences, files over 500 lines. List the 5 largest files.

## Step 8 — Potential Issues

Check for: hardcoded secrets/passwords, cleartext HTTP URLs, sensitive data in logs, unsafe WebView config, `!!` crash risks, `runBlocking` on main thread, static Context references (memory leaks), outdated/legacy libraries (RxJava, AsyncTask, support library instead of AndroidX).

## Output Report Structure

```
## 📱 Project Overview         — SDK versions, language, file counts
## 🏗️ Architecture             — pattern, layers, DI, navigation, async
## ✨ Features                  — table: feature / entry point / package
## 📦 Dependencies             — tables per category with versions
## 📊 Code Quality             — metrics table + top 5 largest files
## ⚠️ Potential Issues         — CRITICAL / HIGH / MEDIUM / INFO
## 💡 Summary & Recommendations — top 5 prioritized action items
```

Always include file paths and line numbers for concrete findings. State "N/A — reason" for any metric that cannot be determined. Do not fabricate dependency versions.
